Z||000007||beer_stud_21||09-26-2001||05:24 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>but even my friends who were so against religion and prayer of any kind are starting to care about people, and are showing more faith in other people and god.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I don't want to start a religion discussion  and this is in no way meant to be a flame but.....  Those of us who don't believe in god...  Err.. let me rephrase that...  Those of us who are not religious... Are no less caring than the average bible beater out there in America!  As far as I'm concerned this conflict is an example of one of the biggest problems with religion, and probably the biggest turn off about it to me.  This whole ordeal has been basically caused by peoples (conflicting) religious beliefs.  Just like what has been going on with the israiles and palistinians.  This sort of thing has been going on for thousands of years... ;-(  I don't have the answer to the problem, but I sure wish all the religious ppl in the world could get along!  (fat chance)  ;-(<P>thanks for listening.<BR>-Marlin||||reg||1
Z||000008||Cliff Pennock||09-26-2001||06:25 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>This whole ordeal has been basically caused by peoples (conflicting) religious beliefs.[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>These attacks were the works of madmen who only use religous as an <B>excuse</B> to do what they do. If there was no religion, these phsycopats would have found another excuse to kill people. Hitler used the Bible as an <B>excuse</B> to kill all Jews while in fact he was trying to create a pure Germanic race and the Jews were unlucky enough to be the first non Germanic race he targeted. Would Hitler not have been defeated, he wouldn't have stopped with the Jews.<P>Muslim extremists use the Qur-ãn as an excuse to kill all non-muslims. But they are in fact nothing more than pshycopats. The phsycopat with the most influence (read: money) becomes their leader.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Fformula88||09-26-2001||10:26 AM||||I totally agree.  These people cannot be labeled as true muslims, or people of the faith!  They have twisted the religion to give justification to their actions!<P>Although religion is deffinately involved in the politics of the middle east, these madmen are running around gaining support to conduct terrorism against the US because of our involvement in their politics and nations<P>Many of the hijackers have already been identified as Saudi's.  There are many others in Saudi Arabia who wouldn't go to such an extreme, but stii dislike the US.  This is from the US stationing troops in SA and our support for the rather unpopular kingdom currently in control of the gov't.  Basically the hatred is a result of our interference in their politics and lives.<P>Fighting terrorism is a good idea, but we may also want to take a hard look at our foreign policy in the middle east too.  Terrorism will always exist with a cause, and we certainly do not want to give more causes!  On the other hand, we also do not want the region to fall into chaos, or have more terrorist supporting gov't scome to power etc etc.  Its a very tough political situation. <P>------------------<BR>John  <BR>AOLIM: Fformula88<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero2m6siga.JPG">       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <P>1988 Fiero Formula<BR>1985 Fiero SE 2M6||||reg||1
Z||000010||EdsB52||09-26-2001||02:05 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by beer_stud_21:<BR><B> I don't want to start a religion discussion  and this is in no way meant to be a flame but.....  Those of us who don't believe in god...  Err.. let me rephrase that...  Those of us who are not religious... Are no less caring than the average bible beater out there in America!  As far as I'm concerned this conflict is an example of one of the biggest problems with religion, and probably the biggest turn off about it to me.  This whole ordeal has been basically caused by peoples (conflicting) religious beliefs.  Just like what has been going on with the israiles and palistinians.  This sort of thing has been going on for thousands of years... ;-(  I don't have the answer to the problem, but I sure wish all the religious ppl in the world could get along!  (fat chance)  ;-(<P>thanks for listening.<BR>-Marlin</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I was thinking this exact sentiment right before I read beer stud's post. I agree with this post in whole, with the addition of what Cliff wrote about fanatics attaching themselves to a coercive devise, religion. My knee-jerk reaction was to think the NY disaster was about religion, but with little reflection it's obviously about fanaticism hiding behind religion.<P>Sidebar about my universal take on religion:<BR>I'm secular and agnostic. If you ask many devote Christians, that translates to hedonist, atheist, or heathen. That's not what secular means or objectively infers, but many Christians like to separate themselves from non-believers much of the time. I can't count the number of long-time friends I've lost to being 'born again.' And Christianity is supposed to breed love and togetherness? <BR>Christianity’s #1 myth:<BR>God's love is unconditional. <BR>No it's not. You believe in God or go straight to fncking hell (plagiarized from "And justice for all"). It's true, there's a condition with an ultimatum. Believe in God or go to hell, with the ultimatum of belief before death. If God were really all loving, he would accept everyone regardless of belief. That's where the coercion is implemented; once you get people to buy into the conditional ultimatum, you can then twist your agenda into controlling other aspects of people's lives. Then you integrate religion into politics and that's the Christian based form of local terrorism we get to enjoy. Issues like homosexuality, premarital cohabitation, abortion, sodomy (which includes sex acts other than hetro missionary position in some jurisdictions, while being recently repealed in others), adultery, and dress and grooming. The ironic thing is that many of the aforementioned behaviors are sex based and many of the offenders are from other than the sectors that are vilified by the religious right (in this case Christians). The preceding statement, just as the following is opinion-based and not a reflection of scientific research. My girlfriend of 10 years registers sex offenders for the Sheriff’s department and has for more than 20 years, I just about have a BS in justice, and I watch many trials, so I feel my experience-based opinion deserves credibility, but am declaring no data. It’s declared by the religious right that these ‘sexual deviants’ are from groups that are pre-established as sexual deviant, such as the myth that pedophiles are generally homosexuals, or that homosexuals have a greater propensity to be pedophiles than heterosexuals. This fallacious link between homosexuality and pedophilism seems to be more of misdirection than a fact. My girlfriend and me have a standing joke that all the sexual deviants (rapists and pedophiles) live in the East valley (rich with Mormons) and the other criminals (murderers, larcenists, etc.) live in Phoenix. The truth that I’ve been able to extrapolate in regard to homosexuality and criminal sexual deviancy is that there is little to no correlation. For every Jeffery Dahmer or John Wayne Gacey someone can present to me, I can present 100 Richard Ramirez’s or the killer of Pauly Klaus.<P>Actually, I just performed an Internet search to reinforce my contention that criminal sexual deviants, especially pedophiles (which are the most despicable criminals IMHO) aren’t usually homosexuals. Take it for what it’s worth: <A HREF="http://www.amarillonet.com/stories/041398/let_gays.shtml" TARGET=_blank>http://www.amarillonet.com/stories/041398/let_gays.shtml</A>      The reinforcement I like is this: “Societal myth aside, scientific examination of the documented facts shows that more than 99 percent of molesters are persons who have adult partners of the opposite sex (Pediatrics, Vol. 94, No. 1, July 1994).”<P>Anyway, the purpose of this long rant and extreme tangent is to dispel two myths. First, the myth that God’s love is unconditional, and second, that the façade created by the Christian religious right that anyone not affiliated with them and people that refuse to accept their beliefs are evil people that have a propensity to commit crimes at a higher rate, unless of course they make the switch to Christianity. I used homosexuality as a premise for which to dispel the major myths of Christianity because it’s the focal point of most Christian-based religions. There are other areas of American life that American Christians want to control, like abortion, but I felt the false correlation between homosexuality and sexual deviancy is the most prolific for my argument.<P>Does God exist? Yes, of course God exists. Proof of that is everywhere; the fact that we have churches on every street, the fact that we are here writing about God, and the presence of the Bible and many other books illustrates God’s existence. But the real truth is that God is a concept. I believe the concept of God is used to coerce people’s actions and behavior, regardless to which God we are referring. Is Jesus Christ a 60’s drug addict looking guy floating around up in the clouds? Of course not. Did God create us in his image? No, we created ‘him’ in our image. If that’s not true, then explain why every race of people has a god that exhibits the appearance of that race. Asians have Buddha, Muslims have Mohammed, and we have our drug addict looking guy. American Hispanics paint Jesus with an olive skin, but follow American Christianity for the most part. Many African Americans I’ve asked that are Christians believe in American Christianity, but believe the image of Jesus is dark skinned. Actually their argument is more formidable than Caucasian-America’s because Israel is closer to Africa than the Caucus Mountains. And if Jesus was a Jewish carpenter, then he was Jewish and would resemble characteristics different from Caucasian’s. Like I said, we create our God in our image, just as all nations or races of people do. Is the concept of God a positive thing? Yes. People that are incarcerated find Jesus in jail. Drug addicts use the concept of Jesus or God to rehabilitate. And many non-criminals use the structure of Jesus, God, and Christianity as a foundation for which to guide their lives. The problem I have with Christianity is when people take it out of context and try to implement religious policies onto the rest of the country through legislation or bias.<P>Now, to bring this topic back to point, I believe the radical faction uses their religion to justify their actions, and that the events of 9-11-01 would have taken place even if they (Arabs) were absent religion, just as the murderers of abortion doctors would still murder, but just need a different rationalization. Now, people are going to flame this, good. Please, try not to write, “you’re just a fnckin ******* ,” or, “you always stir up trouble.” Er, uh, you are just all wrong and stuff and Cliff should kick you out of the forum. Hey let’s start a petition to get Ed kicked out. See, if you do that, you prove my point about my criticism of Christianity; BELIVE AS I BELIVE, OR BE REJECTED BY SOCIETY AND REFUSED ENTRANCE INTO OUR VERSION OF SANCTUARY FOR ETERNAL LIFE. Because why would you be angry unless you’re Christian? So if you flame, then you’ve bought into the big lie. If you wish to appear as intelligent, then write a cogent response with opinion or fact. ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Black88GT||09-26-2001||02:30 PM||||Ed, I didnt get to read all of that but judging from what I did read, you have a very distorted belief of basic chrisitan values.  Also, its tough to even say "christian" values because, like our muslim and jewish counterparts, different christian groups interpet the Bible/Jesus' teachings differently (i.e. the shiite muslims are different from the kurds and Baptists are different from Catholics, while it may be a slight difference you get the point)<P>My advice would be to sit down and have a long chat with an educated theologian instead of telling us about chrisitan values.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000012||EdsB52||09-26-2001||03:04 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Black88GT:<BR><B>Ed, I didnt get to read all of that but judging from what I did read, you have a very distorted belief of basic chrisitan values.  Also, its tough to even say "christian" values because, like our muslim and jewish counterparts, different christian groups interpet the Bible/Jesus' teachings differently (i.e. the shiite muslims are different from the kurds and Baptists are different from Catholics, while it may be a slight difference you get the point)<P>My advice would be to sit down and have a long chat with an educated theologian instead of telling us about chrisitan values.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>So what you're saying is I should be fully read before I comment on an entire sect in our society. Then you write, "Ed, I didnt get to read all of that but judging from what I did read, you have a very distorted belief of basic chrisitan values." Why don't you read the entire very wordy post and refute all or some of the elements within that post instead of throwing a blanket over it that I must be misinformed? By your response it's obvious to me that you didn't read the entire post because I wasn't talking about the interpretations of the Bible by different forms of Christianity (Baptists, Catholics, Lutherans, etc). Also, I wasn't comparing Christianity to Muslims to Buddhists. Don't pick out what you want, refute the specific elements. I don't hate Christians aor Christianity and am glad the right to practice whatever religion you want is part of the First Amendment. The problem I have is when Christianity legislates morality.||||reg||1
Z||000013||beer_stud_21||09-26-2001||03:38 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>Basically the hatred is a result of our interference in their politics and lives.<P>Fighting terrorism is a good idea, but we may also want to take a hard look at our foreign policy in the middle east too. Terrorism will always exist with a cause, and we certainly do not want to give more causes! On the other hand, we also do not want the region to fall into chaos, or have more terrorist supporting gov't scome to power etc etc. Its a very tough political situation.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I totally agree!  I did not mean to imply that I think these terrorists are using islam as an excuse to do what they did.  I know they don't stand for the mainstream.  But the reason our foreign policy goes over so poorly in arab countries is because of the differences in our beliefs.  These terrorists hate the US because we try to impose our values on their countrymen (and more importantly women)  Which threatens what they believe in.  So when we come into a country like pakistan or afganistan and try to promote womens rights, or just the fact that women have (relitively) equal rights in our country infuryates psycos like Bin Laden.  I hope that makes sense.  <BR>I don't see much of a difference between these terrorists and the people who assinate "womens health care workers" They are both extremist type people who don't represent a majority but who still go to great extremes to enforce their beliefs.  <P>Regardless, its just MHO.....<P>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>there's a condition with an ultimatum. Believe in God or go to hell, with the ultimatum of belief before death. If God were really all loving, he would accept everyone regardless of belief.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I also believe this.  <P>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>But the real truth is that God is a concept. I believe the concept of God is used to coerce people’s actions and behavior, regardless to which God we are referring.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>And this too... But I don't like discussing this stuff, especially with ppl on the internet because all it does is burn bridges, and I don't want to burn bridges with any of you.....<P>-Marlin<p>[This message has been edited by beer_stud_21 (edited 09-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||Black88GT||09-26-2001||06:45 PM||||Ed.<P>You say "Christianity’s #1 myth:<BR>God's love is unconditional. <BR>No it's not.  You believe in God or go straight to fncking hell blah blah blah blah  "<P>Thats enough B.S. I need to read.  I neither have the time nor the inclination to read the ramblings following that statement.  That shows me right there you know nothing about Christian philosophy, heaven/hell or who goes where.<P>Also, about referring to Jesus as a "drug head looking guy" Thats probably the dumbest thing I have ever heard on this forum (no, I'm not exaggerating, it IS the dumbest).  Do you not understand that EVERYONE back in that time period had long hair and beards?  Does that make them "drug head looking".<P>Also, are you saying that premarital sex is a good thing?  Yeah its great that we have millions of single mothers on welfare.  I just love putting my tax dollars towards them.  Even better is all the tax money spent on STD's, research/education/treatment etc.  I guess we are lucky we don't have the evil Christian terrorists in power or we might have less people on welfare! Whew.<P>About Jesus' race.  It's obvious being Jewish he was not dark skinned.  While many Jews aren't blonde hair blue eyed either, they are obviously not from Africa.  Just because it happens to be around Africa in this case doesn't mean anything since the Jews have been in that region practically forever.  The point is, we (christians) didn't create him in our image.  He was Jewish therefore not like Mohammed which was of darker skin.<P><BR>p.s. read what you wrote.  You continuously characterize ALL christians as "the religious right".  That was my point in saying there is a difference between Catholics and Baptists etc.  No christian I have ever met believes anything Falwell or those other clowns like to pass off as Christianity.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Black88GT (edited 09-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||beer_stud_21||09-26-2001||09:27 PM||||This is why I don't like discussing religion with people, especially ppl on the net.  I have religious friends, christian and mormon.  They are the nicest people, but when the topic of religion comes up they get to be totally bullheaded and stubborn to any new or different ideas.  But thats because they have been conditioned to reject and idea that does not follow their specific beliefs.<P>This is probably why Black88GT sounds flat angry in his posts.  I'm also guessing its because of bad blood between him and edsB52...  I won't pretend to know all of the reasons people on here hate eds, but lets not attack anything and everything he has to say just because it came from him.  It is his <B>opinion</B> afterall!!! <P>-Marlin||||reg||1
Z||000016||My7Fieros||09-26-2001||10:26 PM||||<B><FONT SIZE="2">WELL, I SEE YET AGAIN WHAT SHOULD BE AN UPLIFTING AND POSITIVE POST HAS GONE TO HELL IN A HANDBASKET...THANKS TO A FEW, NAMELY ED.<BR>ED, I HAVE A QUESTION, WHY DO YOU EVEN BELONG TO THIS "FIERO FORUM"? I RARELY, IF EVER, SEE YOU POST ANYTHING FIERO RELATED..ALWAYS OFF TOPIC. I GUESS YOU BEAT ME, YOU FINALLY DID IT. IM JUST GONNA STAY OUT OF O/T SECTION FROM NOW ON. PUNK </B></FONT><BR>------------------<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><FONT COLOR="red"><B><I>My 7 Fieros</FONT></FONT></B></I><P><p>[This message has been edited by My7Fieros (edited 09-26-2001).]||||reg||8
Z||000017||EdsB52||09-27-2001||03:17 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by beer_stud_21:<BR><B>This is why I don't like discussing religion with people, especially ppl on the net.  I have religious friends, christian and mormon.  They are the nicest people, but when the topic of religion comes up they get to be totally bullheaded and stubborn to any new or different ideas.  But thats because they have been conditioned to reject and idea that does not follow their specific beliefs.<P>This is probably why Black88GT sounds flat angry in his posts.  I'm also guessing its because of bad blood between him and edsB52...  I won't pretend to know all of the reasons people on here hate eds, but lets not attack anything and everything he has to say just because it came from him.  It is his [b]opinion</B> afterall!!! <P>-Marlin[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>"But thats because they have been conditioned to reject and idea that does not follow their specific beliefs."<P>Great statement. I prefer to paraphrase with brainwash, but "conditioned" is more clinical.<P>"I'm also guessing its because of bad blood between him and edsB52..."<P>Actually Black88GT and me have gotten along very well for the entire time we've been involved with this forum. Religion must be his Achilles heel. He still hasn't crossed the line with me and I respect him, not that he's hanging his existence upon that. I just wish he could rationally argue his opinion and reinforce it with fact. Black88GT is angry by his reply, but hasn't addressed any of my points yet. I'm not saying Christianity is evil, I'm saying it's coercive and I've illustrated that contention with some valid opinions and an Internet reference. I wish he would attempt to impeach my contentions instead of angrily dismissing it without cogent argument; I know he's able.<P>"I won't pretend to know all of the reasons people on here hate eds, but lets not attack anything and everything he has to say just because it came from him. "<P>I don't think that many people hate me. I think the few that do are consistent and redundant, which gives the appearance of volume and quantity. Good point. The #1 fallacy in argument is to impeach the person's popularity, as opposed to impeaching their premise as is most professional. In other words, a person like Charles Manson might have some good ideas, but due to unpopularity he would be rejected. Bad example, but you get the idea. I think the Christian sector realizes my assertions are irrefutable, that's why the 2 replies I received garnered nothing but angry rhetoric. Please, I challenge someone to respond with intelligent arguments.||||reg||1
Z||000018||EdsB52||09-27-2001||04:13 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by My7Fieros:<BR><B>[b]<FONT SIZE="2">WELL, I SEE YET AGAIN WHAT SHOULD BE AN UPLIFTING AND POSITIVE POST HAS GONE TO HELL IN A HANDBASKET...THANKS TO A FEW, NAMELY ED.<BR>ED, I HAVE A QUESTION, WHY DO YOU EVEN BELONG TO THIS "FIERO FORUM"? I RARELY, IF EVER, SEE YOU POST ANYTHING FIERO RELATED..ALWAYS OFF TOPIC. I GUESS YOU BEAT ME, YOU FINALLY DID IT. IM JUST GONNA STAY OUT OF O/T SECTION FROM NOW ON. PUNK </B></FONT>[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hey, let's all be sphincters and type in upper case bold. I guess that helps diminish his obscure self-image. <P>"WELL, I SEE YET AGAIN WHAT SHOULD BE AN UPLIFTING AND POSITIVE POST HAS GONE TO HELL IN A HANDBASKET...THANKS TO A FEW, NAMELY ED."<P>Having religion shoved down my throat is not the idea of positivism.<P>"Four thousand gathered for mid-day prayer in a downtown cathedral. A New York City church, filled and emptied six times last Tuesday."   <BR>"Republicans stood next to Democrats." (Inferring Democrats should be privileged)   <BR>"Catholics prayed with Jews."<BR>"Knees are bent." [Or was this a reference about the Mustang Ranch (oh, different post)]<BR>"Could this unselfish prayerfulness be the way God intended for us to live all along? Maybe this, in his eyes, is the way we are called to live." (I thought “prayerfulness” was a Bush word until I looked it up!)<BR>"We can resolve to pray more. And we can resolve that, God being our helper, we'll never go back to normal again."<P>Again, the general message is great and I'm not raining on it. The part about the not going back to normal is a statement about a renewed or raised conscious, in my interpretation and I like it. But is it possible that other people are offended by being continually bombarded with messages inferring only good people are Christians, others are evil sinners? And some will say religion is inspiring. I say it's limiting and intrusive. Again, I liked the general tone of your message and thought the sentiments were genuine, minus the religious references.<P>“ED, I HAVE A QUESTION, WHY DO YOU EVEN BELONG TO THIS "FIERO FORUM"?”<P>Like I just wrote: limiting and intrusive. Bible-thumpers require a strict pecking order, like the one within their church organizations and even their families. So to question an individual’s presence in a club is the protocol I would expect from a rigid churchgoer when his beliefs were challenged. Many secular people would react by asking for a better argument, whereas Bible-thumpers react with, “How dare you.” I’ll bet you buy into the fallacy that elders should be respected merely because of their age. What a cop-out.<P>“I RARELY, IF EVER, SEE YOU POST ANYTHING FIERO RELATED..ALWAYS OFF TOPIC.”<P>I’ve been posting about 3-5 per day in the mall and/or technical section. Besides, even if you were correct, who cares? Again, you’re playing the forum Nazi. Limiting and intrusive.<P>“I GUESS YOU BEAT ME, YOU FINALLY DID IT. IM JUST GONNA STAY OUT OF O/T SECTION FROM NOW ON.”<P>Credit me with something worthwhile next time. Oh, and you won’t be able to stay away. Maybe you’ll come back as another forum name, like sjp777 did. Hey I’ve got an idea, how about “Fiero God,” or “Honk your Fiero’s horn if you love Jesus.” Too many words. Hey the Fiero emblem is modeled after the Pegasus, the Pegasus is derived from Greek mythology, and the concept of God and Jesus is as reliable as Greek Mythology.  (Scratches head)    Maybe you could have a moniker of “Jesus-on-Pegasus” and have a signature of a longhaired hippie riding on the back of a Pegasus while it’s flying away. I promise, I won’t be hip to the fact it’s you in disguise. I won’t make the connection, so go ahead and do it so you can reply back in the off-topic section you martyr wanna-be. It’s fitting you would attempt the martyr role here; what’s next, a reference about the Roman Coliseum?<P>“PUNK”<P>Written like a real true-blue Bible-thumper. Between this and doinking your neighbors wife, I think you’ll be excused in church this Sunday. Might as well go ahead and take some more shots, since you’re going for the package-rate tithe this Sunday.<p>[This message has been edited by EdsB52 (edited 09-27-2001).]||||reg||1
